  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Seasonable and drier conditions Tues through \nWed night with unseasonably warm weather returning late week \ninto the weekend.\n\nA surface cold front is still slowly working its way through \nthe lower Ohio Valley and northern Mid-Atlantic based on the 06z\nobservations. Ahead of this front however, a pre-frontal \ntrough, roughly around the I-95 corridor, is slowly ushering in \nslightly drier air across the Piedmont and slowly advancing \neastward. Model guidance continues to push the cold front into \nand through our region from the north-northeast as a backdoor \ncold front through peak heating into this evening. Thankfully, \ndrier air mixing down to the surface behind the pre-frontal \ntrough (mean mixing-ratio at 00z GSO RAOB 13.4 compared to 17.7 \nat MHX) will greatly limit instability along and ahead of the \nfront this afternoon. Isolated sprinkles or a shower or two \ncan't be ruled out, especially across the Sandhills and \nsouthern/central Coastal Plain where favorable diurnal timing, \n500-1500 J/kg of SBCAPE, and glancing influence from trough axis\npivoting across and offshore of the northern Mid-Atlantic may \novercome marginal instability and drier mid/upper-lvl air. \n\nA prolonged period of minimal convective chances is expected \nbehind the frontal passage as a seasonably significant mid/upper\nlvl subtropical ridge (+2.5 to 3 standardized height anomalies \nfrom 850 to 200mb Tues through Thurs night) builds over the TN \nValley and southern Appalachians; perhaps even reaching 600 dam\nat H5 by Wed. A brief dip down to seasonable temperatures is \nexpected post cold-frontal passage Tues and perhaps lingering \ninto Wed before moderating back to 10-15 degrees above normal as\nthe ridge flattens and anomalous H85 temps sag south into the \nCarolinas into the weekend. Forecast uncertainty remains on when\nconvective chances return to the region as how/when the \nanomalous ridge loosens its grip over the Carolinas remains \nhighly inconsistent among NWP models; below normal PoPs may \nlinger until late this weekend into early next week.",
